# Rules Maintenance (LLM Rulebook)

## Purpose

Procedures for maintaining cursor rules repository. Apply when adding, updating, or removing rule files.

## Adding New Rules (MUST)

### 1. Create Rule File

- Follow naming convention: `NNN-topic-name.mdc`
- Use appropriate prefix: 3xx (common), 4xx (ops), 5xx (python), 6xx (nextjs)
- Follow structure from `common/301-cursor-rules-development.mdc`

### 2. Discover All Rules

Run from repository root:

```bash
find cursor-rules -name "*.mdc" -type f | sort
```

Or:

```bash
ls -R cursor-rules/**/*.mdc
```

### 3. Update Master Files

Update in order:

1. Category master (e.g., `python/500-python-master.mdc`) — add to navigation table
2. Root master (`000-master.mdc`) — add to category table

### 4. Verify Structure

```bash
# List all rule files with line counts
find cursor-rules -name "*.mdc" -exec wc -l {} \; | sort -n

# Check for orphaned files (not in any master)
diff <(find cursor-rules -name "*.mdc" | sort) <(grep -h "\.mdc" cursor-rules/*-master.mdc cursor-rules/000-master.mdc | grep -oE "[a-z]+/[0-9]+-[a-z-]+\.mdc" | sort -u)
```

## Prefix Allocation

| Range | Category | Description |
|-------|----------|-------------|
| 000-099 | Root | Repository-level files |
| 300-399 | Common | Cross-cutting rules |
| 400-499 | Ops | Infrastructure, deployment |
| 500-599 | Python | Python development |
| 600-699 | Next.js | Next.js development |
| 700-799 | Reserved | Future use |
| 800-899 | Reserved | Future use |
| 900-999 | Tools | Tooling-specific rules |

## File Checklist

Before committing new rule:

- [ ] File follows `NNN-topic-name.mdc` naming
- [ ] Frontmatter has correct `alwaysApply` value
- [ ] Purpose section defines what, when to apply, when NOT to apply
- [ ] Content follows `common/302-llm-oriented-writing.mdc`
- [ ] Category master updated with new file
- [ ] Root master (`000-master.mdc`) updated
- [ ] No duplicate content with existing rules

## Removing Rules

1. Remove file
2. Update category master — remove from table
3. Update root master — remove from table
4. Search for cross-references in other rules
5. Update or remove cross-references
